WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.359 --> 00:00:03.479
Dear Fellow Scholars, this is Two Minute Papers
with Károly Zsolnai-Fehér.

00:00:03.479 --> 00:00:09.780
A few years ago, the Generative Adversarial
Network architecture appeared that contains

00:00:09.780 --> 00:00:13.889
two neural networks that try to outcompete
each other.

00:00:13.889 --> 00:00:19.090
It has been used extensively for image generation,
and has become a research subfield of its

00:00:19.090 --> 00:00:20.090
own.

00:00:20.090 --> 00:00:25.760
For instance, they can generate faces of people
that don’t exist and much, much more.

00:00:25.760 --> 00:00:30.090
This is great, we should be grateful to live
in a time when breakthroughs like this happen

00:00:30.090 --> 00:00:31.530
in AI research.

00:00:31.530 --> 00:00:35.981
However, we should also note that artists
usually have a vision of the work that they

00:00:35.981 --> 00:00:41.100
would like to create, and instead of just
getting a deluge of new images, most of them

00:00:41.100 --> 00:00:45.840
would prefer to have some sort of artistic
control over the results.

00:00:45.840 --> 00:00:50.170
This work offers something that they call
semantic paint brushes.

00:00:50.170 --> 00:00:55.310
This means that we can paint not in terms
of colors, but in terms of concepts.

00:00:55.310 --> 00:01:00.390
Now this may sound a little nebulous, so if
you look here, you see that as a result, we

00:01:00.390 --> 00:01:06.210
can grow trees, change buildings, and do all
kinds of shenanigans without requiring us

00:01:06.210 --> 00:01:09.660
to be able to draw the results by hand.

00:01:09.660 --> 00:01:12.010
Look at those marvelous results!

00:01:12.010 --> 00:01:16.010
It works by compressing down these images
into a latent space.

00:01:16.010 --> 00:01:21.970
This is a representation that is quite sparse
and captures the essence of these images.

00:01:21.970 --> 00:01:27.100
One of the key ideas is that this can then
be reconstructed by a generator neural network

00:01:27.100 --> 00:01:33.130
to get a similar image back, however, the
twist is that while we are in the latent domain,

00:01:33.130 --> 00:01:38.659
we can apply these intuitive edits to this
image, so when the generator step takes place,

00:01:38.659 --> 00:01:40.890
it will carry through our changes.

00:01:40.890 --> 00:01:45.490
If you look at the paper, you will see that
just using one generator network doesn’t

00:01:45.490 --> 00:01:51.009
yield these great results, therefore this
generator needs to be specific to the image

00:01:51.009 --> 00:01:52.960
we are currently editing.

00:01:52.960 --> 00:01:58.320
The included user study shows that the new
method is preferred over the previous techniques.

00:01:58.320 --> 00:02:03.170
Now, like all of these methods, this is not
without limitations.

00:02:03.170 --> 00:02:07.880
Here you see that despite trying to remove
the chairs from the scene, amusingly, we get

00:02:07.880 --> 00:02:08.890
them right back.

00:02:08.890 --> 00:02:14.630
That’s a bunch of chairs free of charge,
in fact, I am not even sure how many chairs

00:02:14.630 --> 00:02:15.660
we got here.

00:02:15.660 --> 00:02:19.940
If you figured that out, make sure to leave
a comment about it, but all in all, that’s

00:02:19.940 --> 00:02:25.349
not what we asked for, and solving this remains
a challenge for the entire family of these

00:02:25.349 --> 00:02:26.349
algorithms.

00:02:26.349 --> 00:02:32.390
And, good news, in fact, when talking about
a paper, probably the best kind of news is

00:02:32.390 --> 00:02:36.280
that you can try it online through a web demo
right now.

00:02:36.280 --> 00:02:40.570
Make sure to try it out and post your results
here if you find anything interesting.

00:02:40.570 --> 00:02:45.510
The authors themselves may also learn something
new from us about interesting new failure

00:02:45.510 --> 00:02:46.510
cases.

00:02:46.510 --> 00:02:49.280
It has happened before in this series.

